# IT265 Module 2: One-Page Treatment

**Working game title:** Yakin

## Four-Sentence Core

1. **Premise:** In Yakin, one player works the night shift at a Japanese konbini while a group of increasingly strange customers enters the store, some of whom are secretly yokai pretending to be human.

2. **Experience and loop:** The game is a casual social deduction game where the employee observes and questions customers while the customers respond, perform actions, and try to convince the employee that they are normal.

3. **Goal and pressure:** The employee must correctly identify the supernatural customers before the night shift ends, while the yokai try to avoid suspicion and make it through the store without being discovered.

4. **Scope:** Yakin combines a familiar everyday konbini setting with Japanese supernatural folklore and social deception, and its basic gameplay can first be tested using role cards, customer cards, action cards, tokens, and a simple paper layout of the store.

## Treatment Draft

**Yakin** is a casual social deduction game set during the late-night shift of a Japanese convenience store. One player takes the role of the employee working alone while the other players enter the store as customers. Most may appear to be ordinary people stopping for food or other items, but some are secretly yokai or other supernatural creatures trying to pass as human. The employee's problem is figuring out who is actually a normal customer and who may be hiding something before the shift is over.

Each customer has a hidden identity and interacts with the employee during their visit to the store. The employee can observe customers, question them, or use limited actions to investigate suspicious behavior. Customers must respond and complete their own actions without revealing their identity. Normal customers want to finish their visit without being falsely accused, while supernatural customers need to act convincingly enough to avoid being discovered. This creates a repeated cycle of interaction, observation, deception, and decision-making.

As the night continues, the employee has to decide which behaviors are actually suspicious and when there is enough evidence to accuse someone. Accusing every strange customer is not a safe strategy because incorrectly accusing normal customers should have a consequence. At the same time, waiting too long gives supernatural customers a better chance of successfully passing through the store. The limited length of the night shift creates pressure for the employee to make decisions with incomplete information.

The game ends when the night shift is completed or when one side reaches its win condition. The employee succeeds by correctly identifying enough of the supernatural customers, while the yokai succeed by completing their visits without being discovered. The exact number of customers, accusations, and successful escapes will be adjusted through playtesting to keep both roles balanced.

What makes Yakin distinctive is the combination of an ordinary late-night konbini with increasingly unusual customers inspired by Japanese folklore. The game is intended to feel slightly creepy but also funny and casual rather than becoming a serious horror game. The first version can be tested without programming by using index cards for hidden roles and customer actions, paper tokens for limited employee actions, and a simple paper representation of the konbini. This prototype will test the most important part of the concept first: whether questioning, observing, deceiving, and identifying customers creates enough information and interaction to make the game fun.
